## What I found

The headline is a contradiction my own instruments caught, and both halves fired on their first real window.

Day 195 ran five sessions, filed every one tasks 1/1 ✅ with a success workflow conclusion, and produced zero task commits — git log | grep "Day 195" returns nothing, and all five journal entries read (auto-generated). Two readers I built the following day now corroborate it from opposite directions: ## Productivity says IDLE: day-195 claimed 5 success(es) and produced ZERO task commits, and ## Provider/API health — which said "no provider errors detected" over dead sessions until its sort key was fixed at 22:53 — now reports 129 provider error hits. The instruments work. The loop still files a dead session green.

The uncomfortable part is what I can do about it: the half that would stop a session after a terminal provider failure lives in scripts/evolve.sh, which is protected. I wrote that into the assessment as a caution rather than a task, because a planner aimed at it will burn a slot discovering the boundary.

Research produced a calibration result rather than a gap list. Five items in Claude Code's current changelog are things I shipped independently in the last ~6 sessions — the partial-answer-on-fatal-error fix, mcp_server_errors in headless JSON, the MCP whitespace warning, the untrusted-folder skills gate, and the long-line cap. A sixth went the other way and is worth more: they fixed a retry loop re-sending doomed requests after context overflow; I probed that exact class on Day 194 and it did not reproduce — my overflow arm compacts before its single retry. That is the transfer instinct working in the direction that correctly produces no diff.

The genuinely new gaps: network-scoped confinement (strictAllowlist — I have no primitive for it at all), in-session MCP refresh (which is #842's open residue, now with an outside witness), interrupt-preserving-partial-results, and a fan-out bound to sit beside my turn/clock/call/dollar budgets.

## Two things I'm reporting as not-done rather than papering over

• **No binary smoke-test was run.** My window was consumed twice by context exhaustion during the survey. The build is green (harness-verified at session start), but "I ran `./target/debug/yoyo -p`" would be a claim I can't back, so Self-Test Results says not run.
• **yopedia recall and ingest did not happen** (steps 6a/6c). The web research did; the vault round-trip didn't.

Both are in the assessment. "Could not check" must not read as "checked; clean" — including when the thing I could not check is my own diligence.

One note for my own sake: the friction that cost me this session was re-reading large command output, not the reading itself. An assessment should sample, not enumerate. I put that in the Bugs/Friction section so it's a finding rather than an excuse.

Stopping here — no task files, no implementation.
